---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const coasterCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '4 + 2.5*sin(0.8*x)', label: 'track height h(x) (m)', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: 'sqrt(2*9.8*(h0 - (4 + 2.5*sin(0.8*x))))', label: 'cart speed v(x) (m/s)', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'h0', label: 'start height h₀', min: 5, max: 14, step: 0.1, value: 9, unit: 'm' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'position x (m)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/foundational/energy-conservation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Energy is the universe's accounting system. A moving object carries <strong>kinetic
    energy</strong>, KE = ½mv²; height carries <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong>,
    PE = mgh. On an ideal (frictionless) track the two trade back and forth, but their sum never
    changes — that is the <em>conservation of energy</em>. The cart below starts at height h₀ from
    rest, so its speed at any point is fixed by how much height it has lost:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two hills, one budget — lower the start height and watch the cart fail the first hill" widget="plotter" config={coasterCfg} />

  <p>
    Try h₀ ≈ 6: the speed curve snaps where the track rises above the start height. That break is
    the conservation law speaking — no energy, no climb. Now watch the reverse: at every valley the
    speed peaks exactly where the height bottoms out.
  </p>

  <h2>Energy is a scalar, and it adds</h2>
  <p>
    Unlike force, energy has no direction — you add energies from every source with plain
    arithmetic. The unit is the joule (J): lifting an apple (≈100 g) one metre takes about 1 J.
    A few benchmarks: a smartphone charge ≈ 10,000 J, a chocolate bar ≈ 1,000,000 J, one lightning
    bolt ≈ 1,000,000,000 J.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Friction doesn't destroy energy.</strong> It converts ordered motion into disordered molecular jiggling — heat. The books still balance.</li>
    <li><strong>Roller-coaster design is this equation.</strong> Designers make the first hill the tallest; every later hill must be shorter.</li>
    <li><strong>Same law everywhere.</strong> Pendulums, planets, chemical bonds and power grids all obey it — which is why physicists trust it more than almost anything.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, College Physics (Work and Kinetic Energy); Feynman, Lectures on Physics, Vol. I.</p>
</TopicPage>
